{
  "name": "Exploring the benefits and consequences of medical tourism in Kenya",
  "parent_id": 203313825234151,
  "group_id": "3564d388-8765-41a9-9c1c-1d009aeff029",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T15:29:25.517Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-08-12T15:11:02.188Z",
  "published_at": "2026-08-12T15:11:02.173Z",
  "id": 203717695594973,
  "uuid": "e56cd21e-8dfc-44d7-bc0d-78b768d7e15c",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "cd7b97a5-ca27-1d4e-9b6d-63a85b75394c",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "4d89f3b9-049d-e0b3-ea33-072e9006aaaf",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            In the recent years, Kenya has seen an ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "increase\nof over 30%",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "http://ktb.go.ke/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Tourism-Performance-2018-Presentation-Final2.pdf",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " in tourism. Factors such as more political\nstability, improved security, increased and better options for flights, as well\nas more captivating marketing techniques have all attributed to the growth in tourism.\nHowever, the potential for growth in sectors like health tourism have not reached their full potential. If tapped into, this sector could continue to increase the number of foreign visitors for years to come, which would have positive effects on the national economy.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Health tourism",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3206745/",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "\nis defined as \"organised travel outside one's local environment for the\nmaintenance, enhancement, or restoration of an individual's well-being in mind\nand body.\" This can include medical tourism, which, according to the World\nHealth Organization (",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "WHO",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.who.int/global_health_histories/seminars/kelley_presentation_medical_tourism.pdf",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "),\nis classified as traveling outside of \"international borders to receive some\nform of medical treatment\" for reasons such as affordability, accessibility,\nand shorter wait times.  ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "             In 2017, the United Nations Conference\non Trade and Development released a ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "report",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.uneca.org/publications/economic-report-africa-2017.",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "\nstating that Kenya is becoming one of Africa's ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "top",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.businessdailyafrica.com/economy/Kenya-takes-the-lead-in-medical-tourism/3946234-4009460-smulr9z/index.html",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "\nbeneficiaries for cross-border travel for medical purposes. This demand\nis opening up a market that has the potential to provide a boost for the economy as\na whole. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            The possibility for growth that health tourism offers\nis multifaceted. In a ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "previous\narticle",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.aku.edu/news/pages/News_Details.aspx?nid=NEWS-001807&fbclid=IwAR2UIbTRXAcotZ5DYNYgEN0b_4uWzpc0Tl0KbPqlLE9-Brx0EfdOl8Cyt1o",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ", the Aga Khan University East Africa Institute reported on the\ndifficulty that many youth face in finding meaningful employment. Due to\nthe lack of job opportunities, many youths do not continue into post-secondary\neducation. However, an increase in demand for specialised medical care must be\nmet with an increase in the supply of these health care professionals.\nTherefore, prospective and current students in post-secondary education may\nhave more incentive to continue and complete their degrees in order to work in\nthis new, up-and-coming field. That being said, this is only possible if\nfunding and access to education are not an issue for the youth in Kenya, unlike\npresent circumstances. There would also need be an improvement in the\ninvestment and infrastructure by the government to allow for this growth. ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            If health tourism is further developed in Kenya, there\nwould be additional collateral benefits from foreigners seeking medical\ntreatment in sectors such as lodging, food services and transportation. There\nwould be a surge of job opportunities and a market for new\nentrepreneurial ideas if funding for these ventures is made available. Not only\nwould this be beneficial for the youth, but for the Kenyan economy as a whole,\nwith the government generating revenues through foreign exchange and fees from\nprovision of ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "visas",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "https://www.uneca.org/publications/economic-report-africa-2017.",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": ".\nThe case of ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "Karen\nhospital",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
                        "href": "http://www.karenhospital.org/medical-tourism-kenya/",
                        "uuid": null,
                        "anchor": null,
                        "custom": {},
                        "target": "_blank",
                        "linktype": "url"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": " is a perfect example, where they are currently accommodating\nmedical tourism by providing treatment packages, visa assistance,\ntransportation, hotel services, language interpreters, and diverse food options. Evidently, the admission of even one patient has a ripple effect by creating business for all of these sectors, but perhaps  the most beneficial result would be the incentive to invest in strengthening healthcare infrastructure, which would have positive implications for all.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            The promotion of health tourism, therefore, can create a\nmutually beneficial environment for both foreigners and Kenyans by further\npromoting Kenya on the international stage while improving accessibility and\ninfrastructure at a local level. In spite of this, it is important to recognise\nthat there are some unintended negative consequences that would result from\nhealth tourism. ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            If the funding was available to\nsupport the increased infrastructure that would be needed to accommodate\nmedical tourism, the medical system would likely be split up into two separate\ntiers - private and public. As most of the tourists would be dealing with the\nprivate tier, there is the potential to lose many of the current healthcare\nprofessionals to this sector. That would leave a struggling public health system\nin shambles and would continue to be very detrimental to those who are already\nunable to afford healthcare. The result of an under-resourced public system\nwould further exacerbate the disparities between socio-economic classes. ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "            Experts project that medical tourism\nwill easily continue to be a viable sector which has the potential to bring a great deal of income\ninto the economy, notably from foreign sources. However, moving forward, it's important\nfor approaches to be carefully analysed in order to understand the consequences\nthat  could be faced by Kenya's most vulnerable populations.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Imara Dhalla\nis an EAI Research Assistant, conducting research on health, youth and identity\nin coastal areas of Kenya.",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
